%S A401063 5,7,11,23,29,43,61,109,127,167,173,199,223,239,349,353,389,419,457,
%T A401063 521,523,557,577,601,631,643,677,733,739,761,811,877,941,1009,1063,
%U A401063 1117,1129,1223,1277,1361,1367,1427,1429,1433,1451,1453,1459,1471,1481,1511,1559,1583,1597,1609,1613,1667,1723,1811,1871,1879
%N A401063 Primes p for which there exists a prime q < p such that 3*((p#/p) / (q#/q)) == -1 mod p, where p# = A034386(p).
%C A401063 Terms such that q=2 are rare: 7, 23, 1613, are there any others?
%C A401063 If for some prime p, there exists a prime q for which not just 3*((p#/p) / (q#/q)) == -1 mod p but also 3*((p#/p) / (q#/q)) == -1 mod p^2, then the product p^3 * q (which is in A397487) satisfies also a necessary (but not sufficient) condition for being included in A373487, like for example is case with pair p=2333, q=2099 here. Coincidentally, in this case, when we set x = p^3 * q = 26653647817663, then A276085(x) is indeed a multiple of A003415(x). Question: Are there any other such pairs besides p=2333, q=2099 that satisfy the == -1 (mod p^2) congruence? At least not among the first 3800 terms of this sequence.
%H A401063 Antti Karttunen, <a href="/A401063/b401063.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..3800</a>
%H A401063 <a href="/index/Pri#primorial_numbers">Index entries for sequences related to primorial numbers</a>.
%e A401063 5 is a term as 3*((A002110(3)/5) / (A002110(2)/3))+1 = 10 = 2*5.
%e A401063 7 is a term a 1 + 3*((2*3*5) / (2#/2))) = 1+3*30 = 91 = 7*13 is a multiple of 7.
%e A401063 2333 is a term because 1 + ((2333#/2333) / (q#/q)) will be a multiple of 2333 when we set q to 2099, as then x = (1+(3*A002110(primepi(2333)-1)/A002110(primepi(2099)-1))) is a multiple of 2333. What is remarkable is that also x mod (2333^2) = 0. See comments.
%o A401063 (PARI) is_A401063_on_p(p) = { my(pr=Mod(3,p)); forstep(k=primepi(p)-1,1,-1, pr *= Mod(prime(k),p); if((p-1)==lift(pr), return(1))); (0); };
%o A401063 forprime(p=2,2^22,if(is_A401063_on_p(p), print1(p,", ")));
%Y A401063 Cf. A002110, A003415, A034386, A276085, A373487, A397487.
%Y A401063 Cf. also A401060, A401061, A400842.
%K A401063 nonn,new
%O A401063 1,1
